  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 335 PM EDT Wednesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Unsettled conditions return Thursday through \nSaturday with daily chances for showers and thunderstorms.\n\nLatest wx analysis shows high pressure over the area providing \npleasant conditions for late August today. Tonight, the high will \nshift offshore as the next frontal boundary approaches with flow \naloft shifting to the SW. Models continue to agree on the front \nmoving slowly and staying to the NW of the area Thursday which will \nsuppress rain chances. The 12z CAMs are rather unimpressive with \nrespect to convection coverage and heavy rain threat, showing very \nscattered showers on Thursday. Therefore, PoPs have been nudged \ndownward, now mostly slight chc across the area with chc in the \npiedmont and far NE NC. The severe threat seems low for Thursday at \nthis time with only 20-25kt of 500mb flow and relatively weak mid-\nlevel lapse rates. The boundary drops SE Thursday night and settles \ninto the eastern Carolinas and SE VA by Friday, but remains rather \ndiffuse. There is a strengthening jet core over New England, but \nthere is some uncertainty as to how well the RRQ of this feature \ninteracts with the surface boundary. Longer range CAMs shows a \nlarger coverage in showers/storms than Thursday, but with varying \nQPF amounts. PoPs have decreased for areas north of I-64, now 25-50% \nand remain 50-75% south of the I-64 corridor. The main threat will \nbe locally heavy rainfall, but a few severe storms with damaging \nwinds cannot be ruled out. WPC maintains a Marginal Risk of \nexcessive rainfall Thursday and Friday, with a Slight Risk creeping \ninto NE NC Friday, but given the setup, the showers/tstms seem \nscattered by nature causing a lower confidence in flooding. The \nfront won't fully be out of the area by Saturday, lingering around \nthe NC/VA state line which could bring additional showers/storms to \nthe SE VA/NE NC area, especially in the afternoon (40-60% PoPs). \n\nSeasonally hot and humid Thursday through Sunday with high \ntemperatures primarily in the mid to upper 80s.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Hot conditions return early next week.\n\nHot conditions return early next week as a 1.5-2 st dev 500mb ridge \nbuilds from the Central Plains to the Southeast. NBM high \ntemperatures Monday through Wednesday are primarily in the lower to \nmid 90s. However, 850mb temperatures of 19-21C and 1000-850mb \nthickness schemes support highs in the mid to upper 90s. Global \nensembles support these temperatures, showing highs in the mid to \nupper 90s. If the hotter temperatures are realized that will mean \ndeeper mixing, which should allow dewpoints to drop into the upper \n60s/around 70F during peak heating. Regardless, this would support \nheat indices into the lower 100s.",
